+# ECON-02B2d6 — Native NPC construction crews
+
+**Date:** 2026-11-01  
+**Source baseline:** ECON-02B2d5 physical structures ZIP  
+**Ownership:** Single Galaxy colony session; **GitHub is storage, not the development/test hub**.
+
+## Implemented
+
+- Add `construction::Crews` with two durable SQLite tables in the **same world database**: construction jobs and NPC worker assignments. The world is still held under the existing exclusive writer lease.
+- The server allocates jobs to real `Simulation::npc_records()` identities. For this vertical slice, eligible workers must be **named, living residents in a built, stocked habitat at the selected outpost**. Existing policy inspectors, community attendants, clinic medics, utility technicians and individually registered workshop operators reserve their resident headcount first. The same NPC cannot be assigned to two jobs (`npc_id` primary key).
+- At most eight resident NPCs share a structural plan. Multiple plans can progress in one colony world. Their individual **site-local grid positions** and collective labor points persist through SQLite restart. Each assigned NPC takes no more than one adjacent walk step per logical tick; route checks call the existing authoritative `Simulation::structure_edge_passable`, including closed doors and walls. CPU-bounded detouring is not an arbitrary factory size limit.
+- Construction begins only after a worker reaches the requested cell, enough actual modules exist, and the plan remains authorized and incomplete. The sole installation path is the established `Plans::step` -> `PersistentWorld::submit/step` command journal. This debits the ship's real modules and produces the same physical fixture, event and mass-conservation receipts as manual building. Two worker-ticks for a floor and four for a wall or door are provisional gameplay pacing values.
+- The NPC scheduler executes on the **colony C++ host's main simulation thread**, not a separate writer. It may produce a committed tick while working; the SGVIEW stream publishes that frame before the ordinary game tick to preserve frame sequence. Finished or cancelled plans stop automatically. Pause, resume and remove-worker controls are available.
+- Same-origin, session-token protected localhost API (`/factory/api/crews`, `crew_assign`, `crew_unassign`, `crew_active`) delegates to host-side owner/ship/site authorization. The UI controls live inside the **existing colony panel** with other structures, as requested.
+
+## Safety and persistence boundaries
+
+- The browser never supplies a trusted player identity, world path, or worker authority. A requested plan and NPC ID are validated against the host's authoritative colony and resident roster.
+- Real NPCs remain accountable to existing habitat needs, work reservations and population counts. The scheduler does not create residents, structures, food, power or modules.
+- Crew routes and work progress are SQLite operational records beside the canonical simulation snapshot, **not yet simulated 3D NPC agents**. Positions begin at the site's local `(0,0)` staging cell and cannot be interpreted as interplanetary transport or actual carried cargo.
+- Materials are debited at the successful build command, not reserved in a hauling inventory. The ship still must satisfy the existing physical construction range checks. A future delivery/warehouse milestone must provide actual NPC load, carry, drop, reserve and reconciliation mechanics.
+- The engine's `structure_edge_passable` pathfinding operates on an abstract 2D site grid. It is not a complete walkability system, and the detour search has a bounded per-tick node budget. No 3D collision, oxygen/pressure isolation, vertical floor transitions or multi-site pathfinding is claimed.
+- The world may advance one additional tick when a worker completes a structural step. The authoritative frame is emitted before continuing the host's normal loop. Durable plan receipts are the recovery source of truth if a crash occurs between a committed world step and worker bookkeeping.
+- OS ownership lease remains the single-world-writer safeguard. Windows native compilation and installer acceptance were **not executed** in this Linux environment.
+
+## Verified testing
+
+1. Dedicated native test `ECON02B2d6_NPCConstructionCrews`: named residents, two-worker plan, invalid worker/owner, true module consumption and mass conservation, wall detour, persistence, cancellation and no further module debit.
+2. Real localhost test `ECON02B2d6_NPCConstruction_HTTP`: genuine Python relay -> C++ game process -> SQLite, forbidden origin, forged token, wrong site, assignment, work completion, double-assignment refusal, pause and restart/resume.
+3. Offline Chromium `scripts/colony_crew_browser_smoke.py`: colony-owned UI buttons for assignment, pause, resume and removal. This test mocks transport; host effects are separately tested under point 2.
+4. Existing offline Chromium `scripts/colony_structures_browser_smoke.py`: passed. Existing JavaScript suite `node --test tests/*.js`: **93/93 passed**.
+5. Full CMake native build: **completed**. CTest non-live/non-relay: **66/66 passed**. CTest live/relay: **24/25 passed in one parallel run**; the one preexisting `galaxy_b70b_live_colony_e2e` failed with an intermittent `no such table: snapshots` during its relay startup, and **passed when rerun alone**. A subsequent all-serial live batch exceeded the execution window and is not represented as a full green run. Together, each of 91 CTest cases has a recorded pass, but not within one uninterrupted all-green run.
+
+## Next milestones
+
+- Move building supplies into **physically local** storage; assign NPC haulers with explicit pickup, carriage, delivery, and failure-safe material reservation without double debits.
+- Carry per-NPC construction duty in the canonical or journaled workforce authority so a worker cannot be reallocated concurrently to another aggregate site job.
+- Integrate real navmeshes/floor access, doors and physical LOS; introduce blocked-site re-routing and job priorities, fatigue, time budgets and crew experience.
+- Add 3D fixture meshes and scene interactions inside the main client, then validate Linux GUI and Windows GUI acceptance on native hosts.
